A||||10||Formula 18||3.4 Question||||1||
Z||000000||Formula 18||09-18-2000||10:36 PM||||Is the 3.4 out of a 1991 Lumina/Z34 the same as a 3.4 out of a camaro? <P>------------------<BR>Brian<BR> -88 Formula||||reg||1
Z||000001||Terrybogin||09-18-2000||10:56 PM||||No||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||09-19-2000||03:40 AM||||I don't know all the differences, but the Lumina was FWD and the Camaro was/is RWD.  Something about one of them having better power but a more difficult installation and the other being easier to install but a little shorter on power?  Do a search.  This subject has come up a lot lately.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||09-19-2000||08:20 AM||||I believe the Lumina engine has aluminum heads and different pistons and will not bolt up to the Fiero intake manifold.  If you put the Fiero heads and intake on it you get around 7 or 7.5:1 compression.<P>The Camaro motor will accept the Fiero intake system and will give you about 20 t0 30 extra hp and a bunch mort torque.<P>Go to Ed Park's page for some info on installing the Camaro engine in a Fiero.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34</A> <P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000004||lou_dias||09-19-2000||09:18 AM||||tHE LUMNINAS and Monte Carlos used to TDOC 3.4 where as the Camaro uses the pushrod IRON heads.  55hp difference between the 2 cars...215 vs 160...||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnnyK||09-20-2000||05:15 PM||||The Camaro 3.4 only has 160hp? I wanted to do a 3.4 swap, but what is the point if the 2.8 has 135-140?? All that for 20 hp..||||reg||1
Z||000006||86SE||09-20-2000||07:19 PM||||Depends, newer 3.4's have 180 HP.  But with the fiero intake system the 160 HP engines will have more than 160 horses.  Either way you still get 200+ ft lbs of torque.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||1
Z||000007||Patrick's Dad||09-20-2000||07:50 PM||||The Z34/GP GT 3.4's were called the TDC's, as in, Twin Dual Cam. IOW, DOHC. They are 24 valve engines that put out 210hp at the crank, as I'm told. Other things I've found out here:<P>They are finicky with timing belts. Expect no more than 60K on a belt. This alarms me a little, as I've never had timing problems at twice that on my 2.8, and I'm not easy on my drivetrain.<P>They require some cutting to make way for the heads and covers. I'm not sure exactly where, but there is definitely a clearance problem.<P>The electronics can be tricky. This seems to be the most debated point on this forum.<P>The engine from the Camaro is a RWD application, and, as such, requires drilling the block to mount the starter on the other side. As stated above, you can get the 3.4 in FWD dress from other applications, such as the current Grand Prix, Grand Am, Malibu, Alero, Montana, Aztek, Slihouette, Venture, ad nauseum. This 60° V6 has been a corporate workhorse since the early days of the compact pickups (S-10's). The most powerful versions of them (stock) seem to be the ones with the latest aluminum heads. The Camaro V6 has iron heads that must be more restrictive. <P>Whatever you do to hop these engines up will, no doubt, enhance them, regardless of initial power, as the general consensus seems to be that the 2.8 is good for 175hp, max, with decent reliability, and without Nitrous.<P>------------------<BR>This should work until my site comes online....   <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/Tmh.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula 18||09-20-2000||10:21 PM||||Ok, I knew the diffrence between the TDOHC and the push-rod but wanted to know which modle it had. More about the latest head? the aer AL versions that flow about 35 percent more air right? I don't think they work with our intake though and I though i read that there aren't distrubiotrs on the newer 3.4 so you have to swap computers too. Aren't they the programable ones though?  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||09-20-2000||11:07 PM||||Yes, the new 3.4s are DIS. No, you don't have to swap computers, etc. GM isn't going to change castings just to fill this or that. The 3.4 DIS blocks, have a plug where the distributor would be. Pull out the plug, the locking plate hole is even threaded for you.<BR>The 3.4 TDC(DOHC, TDOC, whatever you want to call it)is a completely different animal, it is not a direct bolt in and go swap. Same thing goes for the FWD 3.4 blocks. They have aluminum heads, splayed valves, and will not work with the Fiero intake. The closest to a bolt in and go swap is the 3.4 RWD version. These came with iron heads(same as Fiero), will work with Fiero electronics, and only require minor modification.<P>Go here, all the information needed is on this site: <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#located" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#located</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierosound||09-22-2000||01:22 AM||||The 3.4 DSI blocks actually have what appears to be the bottom end of a distributor in there.  This is required because you still need to drive the oil pump somehow from the gear on the camshaft.  I am the proud owner of one now...  have no use for it of course...||||reg||1
